/* AI brain Q&A pack 07: animals, sports, arts & fun */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["tell me about lions | lion facts | facts about lions | what are lions like", "Lions are big cats that live in groups called **prides**, usually a few related females, their cubs and one or more males. The females do most of the hunting, often working together at night. A male's roar can be heard up to about 8 km (5 miles) away. Most wild lions live in the grasslands of Africa, with a small group in India."],
  ["tell me about tigers | tiger facts | facts about tigers | what are tigers like", "Tigers are the largest wild cats in the world. They live alone in forests and grasslands across Asia and hunt mostly at night by sneaking up on prey. Every tiger has a unique stripe pattern, a bit like a fingerprint, and unlike most cats they enjoy swimming."],
  ["tell me about elephants | elephant facts | facts about elephants | what are elephants like", "Elephants are the largest land animals. There are African and Asian elephants, and both use their trunks to breathe, smell, drink, grab food and greet each other. They live in close family herds led by an older female called the **matriarch**, and they are known for their excellent memory."],
  ["tell me about giraffes | giraffe facts | facts about giraffes | how tall are giraffes", "Giraffes are the tallest animals on Earth, with adults reaching around 5 to 6 m (16 to 19 ft). Even with that long neck they have only seven neck bones, the same number as humans. They use their long, dark tongues to pull leaves off thorny acacia trees, and they only need a few hours of sleep a day."],
  ["tell me about dolphins | dolphin facts | facts about dolphins | are dolphins smart", "Dolphins are very intelligent marine mammals that breathe air through a blowhole on top of their heads. They live in groups called **pods**, talk to each other with clicks and whistles, and find food using **echolocation** (bouncing sound off objects). Many dolphins even have signature whistles that work like names."],
  ["tell me about sharks | shark facts | facts about sharks | are sharks dangerous", "Sharks are fish with skeletons made of **cartilage** instead of bone. There are more than 500 species, from the tiny dwarf lanternshark to the huge whale shark. They can lose and regrow thousands of teeth in a lifetime. Attacks on people are very rare, and sharks are much more at risk from humans than we are from them."],
  ["tell me about whales | whale facts | facts about whales | are whales fish", "Whales are mammals, not fish: they breathe air, are warm-blooded and feed their babies milk. There are two main groups: **baleen whales** (like blue and humpback whales) that filter tiny food from the water, and **toothed whales** (like sperm whales and orcas) that hunt. Humpback whales are famous for their long, complex songs."],
  ["tell me about octopuses | octopus facts | facts about octopus | how smart is an octopus", "Octopuses are amazingly clever sea creatures with eight arms, three hearts and blue blood. They can change color and texture in a split second to hide, squeeze through any gap bigger than their beak, and solve puzzles like opening jars. Each arm has lots of its own nerve cells, so the arms can almost think for themselves."],
  ["tell me about penguins | penguin facts | facts about penguins | can penguins fly", "Penguins are birds that cannot fly, but they are superb swimmers that use their wings like flippers. Almost all penguins live in the Southern Hemisphere, from icy Antarctica to warm places like the Galapagos Islands. Emperor penguins are the biggest, and the dads keep the egg warm on their feet through the winter."],
  ["tell me about owls | owl facts | facts about owls | can owls turn their heads all the way around", "Owls are birds of prey that mostly hunt at night. Their special soft feathers let them fly almost silently, and their excellent hearing helps them find prey in the dark. They can't spin their heads all the way around, but they can turn them about 270 degrees because their eyes are fixed in place."],
  ["tell me about bees | bee facts | facts about bees | why are bees important", "Bees are insects that feed on flower nectar and pollen. As they move from flower to flower they spread pollen, which helps plants make fruit and seeds, so many of the foods we eat depend on them. Honeybees live in hives with one queen, thousands of female workers and some male drones, and they tell each other where food is with a **waggle dance**."],
  ["tell me about ants | ant facts | facts about ants | how strong are ants", "Ants are social insects that live in colonies, sometimes with millions of members. Each colony usually has a queen who lays eggs, while workers find food, care for young and defend the nest. Ants talk to each other using chemical smells called pheromones, and they can carry many times their own body weight."],
  ["tell me about spiders | spider facts | facts about spiders | are spiders insects", "Spiders are **arachnids**, not insects: they have eight legs and two main body parts, while insects have six legs and three. Most spiders make silk, which is incredibly strong for its weight, and many build webs to catch food. They are helpful to people because they eat lots of pests like flies and mosquitoes."],
  ["tell me about snakes | snake facts | facts about snakes | how do snakes move without legs", "Snakes are legless reptiles that move by pushing their scales and muscles against the ground. They smell by flicking their forked tongue to collect scent particles from the air. Snakes swallow their food whole, and some can open their jaws wide enough to eat prey bigger than their heads. Most snakes are harmless to people."],
  ["tell me about frogs | frog facts | facts about frogs | what is the difference between a frog and a toad", "Frogs are **amphibians**, which means they usually start life in water as tadpoles and then grow legs to live on land. They can breathe and drink through their skin, so they need to stay moist. Toads are a kind of frog that usually have drier, bumpier skin and shorter legs for hopping rather than leaping."],
  ["tell me about cats | cat facts | facts about cats | facts about house cats", "Pet cats are skilled little hunters with sharp senses: they can see well in dim light, hear very high sounds and land on their feet thanks to their **righting reflex**. They sleep a lot, often 12 to 16 hours a day. Cats communicate with meows, purrs, tail positions and slow blinks, which are a sign of trust."],
  ["tell me about dogs | dog facts | facts about dogs | why are dogs called man's best friend", "Dogs were the first animals people tamed, many thousands of years ago, and they descend from wolves. Their sense of smell is many thousands of times better than ours, which is why they help with search, rescue and detection work. There are hundreds of breeds, from tiny Chihuahuas to giant Great Danes, and they are loyal, social companions."],
  ["tell me about horses | horse facts | facts about horses | can horses sleep standing up", "Horses are strong, fast animals that have worked alongside people for thousands of years. They can doze standing up thanks to a special locking system in their legs, but they lie down for deep sleep. Horses have the largest eyes of any land mammal, and their height is traditionally measured in **hands** (one hand is 4 inches)."],
  ["tell me about pandas | panda facts | facts about pandas | facts about giant pandas", "Giant pandas are bears that live in the mountain forests of central China. Their diet is almost all bamboo, and because bamboo isn't very nutritious they spend up to 14 hours a day eating. They have a special wrist bone that works like a thumb for gripping stalks. Baby pandas are born tiny, about the size of a stick of butter."],
  ["tell me about koalas | koala facts | facts about koalas | are koalas bears", "Koalas are not bears; they are **marsupials** from Australia that carry their babies (called joeys) in a pouch. They eat eucalyptus leaves, which are tough and low in energy, so koalas sleep around 18 to 22 hours a day to save energy. They get most of the water they need from the leaves."],
  ["tell me about kangaroos | kangaroo facts | facts about kangaroos | how far can a kangaroo jump", "Kangaroos are marsupials from Australia that hop on powerful back legs and use their thick tail for balance. Big red kangaroos can cover around 8 m (25 ft) in a single bound and reach speeds of about 55 km/h (35 mph). A baby kangaroo, called a joey, grows inside its mother's pouch for months."],
  ["tell me about cheetahs | cheetah facts | facts about cheetahs | how fast is a cheetah", "Cheetahs are the fastest land animals, able to sprint at around 100 to 120 km/h (60 to 75 mph) for short bursts. Their slim bodies, long legs, flexible spine and long tail for steering are all built for speed. Unlike lions and tigers, cheetahs can't roar; they chirp, purr and growl instead."],
  ["tell me about bats | bat facts | facts about bats | are bats blind", "Bats are the only mammals that can truly fly. They are not blind, but many species hunt at night using **echolocation**, sending out high-pitched calls and listening for the echoes. Some bats eat insects (lots of them!), while others eat fruit and nectar and help pollinate plants and spread seeds."],
  ["tell me about butterflies | butterfly facts | facts about butterflies | how does a caterpillar become a butterfly", "Butterflies are insects with four colorful wings covered in tiny scales. They go through **metamorphosis**: egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, then adult butterfly. They taste with their feet and drink nectar through a long, curled tube called a proboscis. Monarch butterflies make an amazing migration of thousands of kilometres."],
  ["tell me about wolves | wolf facts | facts about wolves | why do wolves howl", "Wolves are wild relatives of dogs that live and hunt in family groups called **packs**. They howl to talk to pack members over long distances, gather the group, and warn other packs to stay away. Wolves are strong travellers and can cover dozens of kilometres in a day looking for food."],
  ["tell me about bears | bear facts | facts about bears | do bears really hibernate", "Bears are large, powerful mammals found in North and South America, Europe and Asia. Many, like black and brown bears, spend winter in a deep sleep-like state in a den, living off stored fat, and mothers even give birth during this time. Bears have an amazing sense of smell and eat a mix of plants, berries, insects, fish and meat."],
  ["tell me about polar bears | polar bear facts | facts about polar bears | why are polar bears white", "Polar bears live around the Arctic and are the largest land carnivores. Their fur looks white, but each hair is actually clear and hollow, and their skin underneath is black to soak up heat. They hunt seals from the sea ice and are excellent swimmers."],
  ["tell me about gorillas | gorilla facts | facts about gorillas | what do gorillas eat", "Gorillas are the largest primates and live in the forests of central Africa. They are mostly gentle plant-eaters, munching on leaves, stems and fruit. Gorillas live in family groups led by a big adult male called a **silverback**, named after the silver hair on his back."],
  ["tell me about chimpanzees | chimpanzee facts | facts about chimps | are chimps related to humans", "Chimpanzees are great apes from Africa and among our closest living relatives, sharing most of our DNA. They are very smart and use tools, like sticks to fish termites out of mounds and stones to crack nuts. Chimps live in communities and communicate with calls, gestures and facial expressions."],
  ["tell me about monkeys | monkey facts | facts about monkeys | what is the difference between a monkey and an ape", "Monkeys are primates found in Africa, Asia and Central and South America. Most monkeys have tails, while apes (like gorillas, chimps and orangutans) don't, and apes are usually bigger with larger brains. Some South American monkeys have **prehensile** tails that can grip branches like an extra hand."],
  ["tell me about zebras | zebra facts | facts about zebras | why do zebras have stripes", "Zebras are African members of the horse family with bold black-and-white stripes, and no two zebras have the same pattern. Scientists think the stripes may help keep biting flies away, and they may also confuse predators when a herd runs together. Zebras live in herds and often graze alongside wildebeest."],
  ["tell me about hippos | hippo facts | facts about hippopotamus | are hippos dangerous", "Hippos are huge African mammals that spend most of the day in rivers and lakes to stay cool, then come out at night to graze on grass. They can't really swim; they walk or bounce along the bottom. Their skin makes a reddish oily liquid that acts like sunscreen. Hippos are very territorial and can be dangerous, so they are best admired from far away."],
  ["tell me about rhinos | rhino facts | facts about rhinoceros | what are rhino horns made of", "Rhinos are large, thick-skinned plant-eaters found in Africa and Asia. Their horns are made of **keratin**, the same material as your hair and fingernails. Rhinos have poor eyesight but a great sense of smell and hearing. Several kinds of rhino are endangered, and many people work hard to protect them."],
  ["tell me about crocodiles | crocodile facts | facts about crocodiles | what is the difference between alligators and crocodiles", "Crocodiles are large reptiles that have been around since the time of the dinosaurs. A simple way to tell them from alligators: crocodiles usually have a narrower, V-shaped snout and their lower teeth show when the mouth is closed, while alligators have a wider, U-shaped snout. Crocodiles have one of the strongest bites of any animal."],
  ["tell me about turtles | turtle facts | facts about turtles | what is the difference between a turtle and a tortoise", "Turtles are reptiles with a shell that is part of their skeleton, so they can't climb out of it. Tortoises are turtles that live on land and have dome-shaped shells and sturdy legs, while sea turtles have flippers for swimming. Some tortoises can live well over 100 years."],
  ["tell me about eagles | eagle facts | facts about eagles | how good is an eagle's eyesight", "Eagles are large, powerful birds of prey with hooked beaks and strong talons. Their eyesight is several times sharper than a human's, so they can spot a rabbit from far up in the sky. Many eagles mate for life and build huge nests, called eyries, that they reuse and add to every year."],
  ["tell me about parrots | parrot facts | facts about parrots | why can parrots talk", "Parrots are colorful, clever birds found mostly in warm parts of the world. Many can copy sounds and human words because they have a special voice organ and strong social learning skills. They have curved beaks for cracking nuts and feet with two toes forward and two back, perfect for gripping. Some large parrots can live 50 years or more."],
  ["tell me about hummingbirds | hummingbird facts | facts about hummingbirds | how fast do hummingbirds flap their wings", "Hummingbirds are tiny birds from the Americas that can hover in midair and are the only birds that can fly backwards. Their wings can beat dozens of times per second, which makes the humming sound. They need lots of energy, so they visit hundreds of flowers a day to sip nectar."],
  ["tell me about flamingos | flamingo facts | facts about flamingos | why are flamingos pink", "Flamingos are wading birds famous for their pink color, which comes from pigments in the algae and tiny shrimp they eat. They're born with grey feathers and turn pink over time. Flamingos often stand on one leg, and they eat with their heads upside down, filtering food from the water with their beaks."],
  ["tell me about jellyfish | jellyfish facts | facts about jellyfish | do jellyfish have brains", "Jellyfish have been drifting in the oceans for hundreds of millions of years, even longer than dinosaurs. They have no brain, heart, bones or blood; instead they have a simple net of nerves. They catch food with stinging tentacles. Jellyfish are mostly water, which is why they look see-through."],
  ["tell me about starfish | starfish facts | facts about sea stars | can starfish regrow arms", "Starfish, also called sea stars, aren't fish at all; they're echinoderms, related to sea urchins. Most have five arms, and many can regrow an arm if they lose one. They have an eye spot at the tip of each arm and move using hundreds of tiny tube feet. Some can push their stomach out of their body to eat."],
  ["tell me about seahorses | seahorse facts | facts about seahorses | do male seahorses have babies", "Seahorses are small fish that swim upright and hold onto seagrass with their curly tails. In a rare twist, the **father** carries the eggs in a pouch and gives birth to the babies. Seahorses can move each eye independently, and they're slow swimmers that rely on camouflage to stay safe."],
  ["tell me about camels | camel facts | facts about camels | what is in a camel's hump", "Camels are desert animals built to handle heat and dry conditions. Their humps store **fat**, not water, which they can use for energy when food is scarce. They can go a long time without drinking and then gulp a huge amount at once. Dromedary camels have one hump and Bactrian camels have two."],
  ["tell me about sloths | sloth facts | facts about sloths | why are sloths so slow", "Sloths live in the rainforests of Central and South America and spend most of their lives hanging upside down in trees. They move slowly because their leafy diet gives them very little energy, so they save it wherever they can. Algae can grow on their fur, giving them a greenish tint that helps them hide. Surprisingly, they're good swimmers."],
  ["tell me about foxes | fox facts | facts about foxes | what does a fox sound like", "Foxes are small members of the dog family with pointed ears and bushy tails. They're clever, adaptable hunters that eat small animals, insects and fruit, and some even live in cities. Foxes make lots of sounds, including barks and loud screams. Red foxes can hear tiny animals moving under the snow and pounce on them."],
  ["tell me about rabbits | rabbit facts | facts about rabbits | why do rabbits twitch their noses", "Rabbits are small plant-eating mammals with long ears and strong back legs. Their teeth never stop growing, so they chew a lot to keep them worn down. They twitch their noses to pick up smells, and a happy rabbit may do a jumping twist in the air called a **binky**. Rabbits are not rodents; they belong to a group called lagomorphs."],
  ["tell me about squirrels | squirrel facts | facts about squirrels | do squirrels remember where they bury nuts", "Squirrels are rodents with bushy tails that they use for balance, warmth and signalling. Many bury nuts to eat later and remember lots of their hiding spots, often finding them with smell too. The ones they forget can grow into new trees, so squirrels help plant forests."],
  ["tell me about mice | mouse facts | facts about mice | facts about rats", "Mice and rats are rodents, which means their front teeth keep growing and must be worn down by gnawing. Mice are small, curious and can squeeze through tiny gaps, while rats are larger and very clever. Both have a great sense of smell and touch, using their whiskers to feel their way in the dark."],
  ["tell me about hamsters | hamster facts | facts about hamsters | why do hamsters stuff their cheeks", "Hamsters are small rodents that are most active at night. They have stretchy cheek pouches that can stretch back to their shoulders, letting them carry food back to their burrow to store. In the wild they dig tunnels, and as pets they love running on wheels to burn off energy."],
  ["tell me about guinea pigs | guinea pig facts | facts about guinea pigs | what do guinea pigs eat", "Guinea pigs are friendly rodents originally from South America, and they're not related to pigs at all. They eat hay, fresh vegetables and pellets, and need vitamin C in their diet because their bodies can't make it. When they're happy they may jump straight up in the air, which is called **popcorning**."],
  ["tell me about goldfish | goldfish facts | facts about goldfish | do goldfish have a 3 second memory", "Goldfish are a type of carp that people have kept as pets for over a thousand years. The idea that they have a 3-second memory is a myth; studies show they can remember things for months and can even be trained. With good care in a big enough tank or pond, goldfish can live 10 years or more."],
  ["tell me about cows | cow facts | facts about cows | why do cows chew cud", "Cows are farm animals raised for milk, meat and leather. They have one stomach with four compartments to digest tough grass, and they bring food back up to chew again, which is called **chewing cud**. Cows are social animals that make friends in their herd and can recognise many faces."],
  ["tell me about pigs | pig facts | facts about pigs | are pigs smart", "Pigs are very intelligent animals, often considered smarter than dogs at some tasks. They roll in mud to cool off and protect their skin from the sun, since they can't sweat much. Pigs have a great sense of smell and use their snouts to dig for food."],
  ["tell me about sheep | sheep facts | facts about sheep | facts about goats", "Sheep are farm animals kept for wool, milk and meat, and their wool keeps growing so they need to be sheared. Goats are close relatives that are curious, great climbers and famous for nibbling almost anything. Both have rectangular pupils that give them a wide view to watch for danger."],
  ["tell me about chickens | chicken facts | facts about chickens | are chickens related to dinosaurs", "Chickens are the most common birds in the world and are kept for eggs and meat. They are distant relatives of dinosaurs; birds actually evolved from a group of dinosaurs. Hens can lay around one egg a day, and chickens communicate with dozens of different sounds."],
  ["tell me about ducks | duck facts | facts about ducks | why don't ducks get wet", "Ducks are water birds with webbed feet for paddling and flat bills for finding food. They stay dry because a gland near their tail makes oil that they spread over their feathers to make them waterproof. Baby ducks, called ducklings, follow the first moving thing they see, usually their mother."],
  ["tell me about orcas | orca facts | facts about killer whales | is a killer whale a whale or a dolphin", "Orcas, or killer whales, are actually the largest members of the **dolphin** family. They are top predators that hunt in family groups called pods, using teamwork and clever tactics. Different groups have their own calls and hunting styles, almost like local cultures."],
  ["tell me about blue whales | blue whale facts | facts about the blue whale | how big is a blue whale", "The blue whale is the largest animal ever known to have lived, bigger than any dinosaur. It can grow to around 30 m (about 100 ft) long, and its heart is about the size of a small car. Despite its size, it eats mostly tiny shrimp-like animals called **krill**, scooping up tonnes of them a day."],
  ["tell me about hedgehogs | hedgehog facts | facts about hedgehogs | why do hedgehogs have spikes", "Hedgehogs are small mammals covered in thousands of stiff spines made of keratin. When frightened they roll into a tight, prickly ball that's hard for predators to bite. They're mostly active at night and eat insects, worms and slugs, which makes them a gardener's friend."],
  ["tell me about raccoons | raccoon facts | facts about raccoons | why do raccoons wash their food", "Raccoons are clever North American mammals with a black mask-like face and ringed tails. Their front paws are super sensitive, and dipping food in water seems to make their sense of touch even better, which is why they look like they're washing it. They're great at opening things, including bins!"],
  ["tell me about otters | otter facts | facts about otters | do otters hold hands", "Otters are playful, water-loving mammals with thick, waterproof fur. Sea otters sometimes hold paws or wrap themselves in kelp while sleeping so they don't drift apart. They use rocks as tools to crack open shellfish, and some keep a favourite rock in a pocket of skin under their arm."],
  ["tell me about beavers | beaver facts | facts about beavers | why do beavers build dams", "Beavers are large rodents famous for building dams from sticks, mud and stones. The dams create ponds that keep them safe from predators and let them build lodges with underwater entrances. Their big orange teeth are strong enough to cut down trees, and their flat tails help them steer and slap the water as a warning."],
  ["tell me about deer | deer facts | facts about deer | do deer lose their antlers", "Deer are graceful plant-eating mammals found on most continents. Male deer usually grow antlers, which are made of bone, fall off every year and grow back even bigger. Reindeer (called caribou in North America) are the only deer where females grow antlers too."],
  ["tell me about peacocks | peacock facts | facts about peacocks | why do peacocks have big tails", "Peacocks are male peafowl, famous for their shimmering tail feathers covered in eye-like spots. They fan out their tails and shake them to impress females, called peahens, which are brown and less flashy. The colors come from tiny structures in the feathers that reflect light, not just pigment."],
  ["tell me about snails | snail facts | facts about snails | why are snails slimy", "Snails are mollusks that carry a spiral shell on their back for protection. They glide along on a single muscular foot and make slime to help them move smoothly and protect their bodies. Many snails have eyes on the tips of their long tentacles."],
  ["tell me about worms | earthworm facts | facts about earthworms | why do worms come out when it rains", "Earthworms are great for the planet: they tunnel through soil, letting in air and water, and their droppings make the soil rich for plants. They breathe through their skin, which must stay moist. Worms often come to the surface when it rains, possibly because it's easier to travel across damp ground."],
  ["tell me about ladybugs | ladybug facts | facts about ladybirds | do ladybug spots tell their age", "Ladybugs (or ladybirds) are small beetles, often red with black spots. Their spots do **not** show their age; the number depends on the species. Gardeners love them because they eat aphids, tiny bugs that harm plants. Their bright color warns predators that they taste bad."],
  ["tell me about dinosaurs | dinosaur facts | facts about dinosaurs | when did dinosaurs live", "Dinosaurs were reptiles that ruled the land for over 160 million years, until most of them died out about 66 million years ago, likely after a giant asteroid hit Earth. Some were huge plant-eaters like Brachiosaurus, while others were fierce hunters like Tyrannosaurus rex. Birds are the living descendants of dinosaurs."],
  ["what is the fastest animal | fastest animal in the world | what animal is the fastest", "The **peregrine falcon** is the fastest animal: when it dives (called a stoop) it can go over 300 km/h (about 200 mph). On land the fastest is the **cheetah**, and in the water fish like the sailfish are among the speediest."],
  ["what is the fastest land animal | fastest animal on land", "The **cheetah** is the fastest land animal, sprinting at around 100 to 120 km/h (60 to 75 mph), but only for short bursts of a few hundred metres."],
  ["what is the fastest fish | fastest animal in the ocean | fastest sea creature", "The **sailfish** and its relatives like the black marlin are usually named the fastest fish, with bursts estimated at over 100 km/h. They use their long bills to slash at schools of smaller fish."],
  ["what is the fastest bird | fastest flying bird", "In a dive, the **peregrine falcon** is the fastest bird by far. In level flapping flight, birds like swifts are among the quickest."],
  ["what is the biggest animal | largest animal in the world | biggest animal ever", "The **blue whale** is the biggest animal that has ever lived, growing to about 30 m (100 ft) long and weighing as much as around 150 tonnes or more."],
  ["what is the biggest land animal | largest land animal", "The **African bush elephant** is the largest land animal. Big males can stand about 3 to 4 m (10 to 13 ft) tall at the shoulder and weigh over 6 tonnes."],
  ["what is the tallest animal | tallest animal in the world", "The **giraffe** is the tallest animal, with adult males reaching up to about 5.5 to 6 m (18 to 19 ft), much of it neck and legs."],
  ["what is the biggest bird | largest bird in the world", "The **ostrich** is the biggest bird. It can be about 2.7 m (9 ft) tall, can't fly, but can run at around 70 km/h (43 mph). It also lays the biggest eggs of any living bird."],
  ["what is the smallest bird | tiniest bird in the world", "The **bee hummingbird** from Cuba is the smallest bird, only about 5 to 6 cm (2 inches) long, roughly the size of a large bee."],
  ["what is the smallest mammal | tiniest mammal", "The smallest mammals are the **bumblebee bat** (also called Kitti's hog-nosed bat) and the **Etruscan shrew**, both weighing only around 2 grams, less than a small coin."],
  ["what is the smallest animal | tiniest animal in the world", "Some of the smallest animals are microscopic creatures like **tardigrades** (water bears) and tiny insects such as fairyflies, which are smaller than a grain of salt. Among animals with backbones, certain tiny frogs and fish are only about 7 to 8 mm long."],
  ["what is the biggest fish | largest fish in the world", "The **whale shark** is the biggest fish, growing to around 12 m (40 ft) or more. Despite its size, it's a gentle filter-feeder that eats plankton and small fish."],
  ["what is the biggest snake | largest snake in the world | longest snake", "The **reticulated python** is the longest snake, sometimes over 6 m (20 ft). The **green anaconda** is the heaviest, with a very thick, powerful body."],
  ["what is the biggest cat | largest cat in the world", "The **tiger** is the largest wild cat species, with Siberian (Amur) tigers being the biggest. Lions come a close second."],
  ["what is the biggest reptile | largest reptile in the world", "The **saltwater crocodile** is the largest living reptile. Big males can grow over 6 m (20 ft) long and weigh more than a tonne."],
  ["what is the biggest insect | largest insect in the world", "It depends how you measure. Some of the heaviest insects are giant weta and Goliath beetles, while some stick insects are the longest, stretching over 50 cm with their legs out."],
  ["what is the biggest spider | largest spider in the world", "The **Goliath birdeater**, a tarantula from South America, is the heaviest spider and can be as big as a dinner plate with its legs spread. The giant huntsman spider has the widest leg span."],
  ["what is the oldest living animal | longest living animal | what animal lives the longest", "Some animals live amazingly long lives. Greenland sharks may live for several hundred years, bowhead whales can live over 200 years, and giant tortoises often pass 100. Some sponges and ocean clams can live even longer."],
  ["what is the slowest animal | slowest animal in the world", "The **three-toed sloth** is one of the slowest mammals, moving at only a few metres a minute. Snails, sea stars and seahorses are famously slow too."],
  ["what animal has the best sense of smell | animal with the best nose", "Elephants have one of the best senses of smell on land, with more smell genes than any animal studied. Bears, bloodhounds and sharks are also champion sniffers."],
  ["what is the loudest animal | loudest animal in the world", "The **sperm whale** makes some of the loudest sounds of any animal, using powerful clicks. On land, howler monkeys can be heard several kilometres away."],
  ["what is the most dangerous animal | deadliest animal in the world", "Surprisingly, the deadliest animal to humans is the **mosquito**, because it spreads diseases like malaria. That's why mosquito nets and repellents are so important in many parts of the world."],
  ["what animal has the longest neck", "The **giraffe** has the longest neck of any living animal, around 1.8 m (6 ft), yet it still has only seven neck bones."],
  ["what animal has the biggest eyes | biggest eyes in the animal kingdom", "The **giant squid** and **colossal squid** have the biggest eyes of any animal, around the size of a dinner plate, helping them see in the dark deep sea."],
  ["what is the strongest animal | strongest animal for its size", "For its size, the **dung beetle** is often called the strongest animal, able to pull many hundreds of times its own weight. For raw strength, elephants are the strongest land animals."],
  ["how many legs does a spider have | do spiders have 8 legs", "Spiders have **8 legs**. That's one way to tell them apart from insects, which have 6."],
  ["how many legs does an insect have | how many legs do bugs have", "All adult insects have **6 legs** and three main body parts: head, thorax and abdomen."],
  ["how many legs does a centipede have | do centipedes have 100 legs", "Despite the name, centipedes don't always have 100 legs. Depending on the species they have anywhere from about 30 to over 350, and always an odd number of leg pairs, so never exactly 100!"],
  ["how many legs does an octopus have | how many arms does an octopus have", "An octopus has **8 arms**. Scientists usually call them arms rather than legs, and each one is covered in suckers that can taste and feel."],
  ["how many hearts does an octopus have", "An octopus has **3 hearts**: two pump blood to the gills and one pumps it to the rest of the body. Its blood is blue because it uses copper to carry oxygen."],
  ["why do cats purr | what does it mean when a cat purrs", "Cats often purr when they're content and relaxed, but they also purr when hungry, nervous or even hurt, so it can be a way of comforting themselves. The sound comes from fast vibrations of muscles in the voice box. Mother cats purr to their kittens too."],
  ["why do dogs wag their tails | what does a dog wagging its tail mean", "Dogs wag their tails to communicate. A loose, wide wag usually means a happy, friendly dog, but a stiff or high, fast wag can mean excitement or nervousness. It's best to look at the whole dog, including its ears and body, to understand how it feels."],
  ["why do cats knead | why do cats make biscuits", "Cats knead (push their paws in and out, also called making biscuits) because kittens do it to their mother to get milk. Grown cats keep doing it when they feel safe and happy, and it also leaves their scent from glands in their paws."],
  ["why do dogs sniff everything | why do dogs smell each other", "Dogs explore the world through their noses. Sniffing tells them who has been around, what they ate and how they're feeling, a bit like reading the news. Sniffing other dogs is their way of saying hello and getting to know each other."],
  ["do fish sleep | how do fish sleep", "Yes, fish rest, though not like we do. Most fish don't have eyelids, so their eyes stay open, but they slow down, float in place or tuck into safe spots to rest. Some parrotfish even wrap themselves in a bubble of mucus at night!"],
  ["do fish drink water | do fish get thirsty", "It depends! Saltwater fish drink seawater and get rid of the extra salt through their gills. Freshwater fish barely drink at all, because water soaks into their bodies through their skin and gills."],
  ["how do fish breathe underwater | can fish breathe", "Fish breathe using **gills**. Water flows in through the mouth and over the gills, which pull oxygen out of the water and into the blood."],
  ["can dogs see color | are dogs color blind", "Dogs aren't completely color blind. They see mostly in blues and yellows and have trouble telling red from green, a bit like a person with red-green color blindness."],
  ["why do birds sing | why do birds chirp in the morning", "Birds sing to attract mates and to tell other birds \"this is my territory.\" Many sing loudest at dawn, called the **dawn chorus**, possibly because the air is calm and sound travels well early in the morning."],
  ["how do birds fly | why can birds fly", "Birds fly thanks to light, hollow bones, strong chest muscles and wings shaped to create lift as air flows over them. Feathers help them steer, and their bodies are built to be as light as possible."],
  ["why do birds migrate | where do birds go in winter", "Many birds migrate to warmer places in winter where there's more food, then return in spring to breed. Some, like the Arctic tern, travel incredible distances, flying from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year."],
  ["why do bears hibernate | what is hibernation", "Hibernation is a deep, long rest some animals use to survive winter when food is hard to find. Their heartbeat, breathing and body temperature slow down to save energy. Hedgehogs, bats and some ground squirrels are true hibernators."],
  ["how do chameleons change color | why do chameleons change color", "Chameleons change color using special skin cells with tiny crystals that reflect light differently when the skin stretches or relaxes. They do it mostly to show their mood, to signal to other chameleons and to help control body temperature, as well as to blend in."],
  ["why do zebras and tigers have stripes | why do animals have stripes and spots", "Stripes and spots can act as camouflage, breaking up an animal's outline in grass or shadows so predators or prey don't notice it. Tigers' stripes help them hide in tall grass, while zebras' stripes may also keep biting flies away."],
  ["what is a mammal | what makes an animal a mammal", "Mammals are warm-blooded animals with backbones that have hair or fur and feed their babies milk. Most give birth to live young. Humans, dogs, whales and bats are all mammals; platypuses and echidnas are unusual mammals that lay eggs."],
  ["what is a reptile | what makes an animal a reptile", "Reptiles are cold-blooded animals with scaly skin that breathe air, and most lay eggs on land. Snakes, lizards, turtles and crocodiles are reptiles. Because they're cold-blooded, they often bask in the sun to warm up."],
  ["what is an amphibian | examples of amphibians", "Amphibians are cold-blooded animals that usually start life in water and later live on land, like frogs, toads, salamanders and newts. They have soft, moist skin that they can breathe through."],
  ["what is an endangered animal | what does endangered mean", "An endangered animal is one at serious risk of dying out in the wild, often because of habitat loss, hunting or pollution. Examples include some tigers, rhinos, orangutans and sea turtles. Protecting habitats and supporting conservation helps them recover."],
  ["what does extinct mean | what animals are extinct", "Extinct means a kind of animal or plant no longer exists anywhere. Famous examples include dinosaurs (apart from birds), the dodo, the woolly mammoth and the passenger pigeon."],
  ["what is a platypus | platypus facts | tell me about the platypus", "The platypus is an unusual Australian mammal with a duck-like bill, a beaver-like tail and webbed feet. It's one of the only mammals that lays eggs, and males have a venomous spur on their back legs. It finds food underwater by sensing tiny electric signals from prey."],
  ["what do you call a group of crows | what is a group of animals called | funny animal group names", "Groups of animals have some fun names! A group of crows is a **murder**, owls are a **parliament**, flamingos are a **flamboyance**, lions are a **pride**, fish are a **school** and jellyfish can be a **smack**."],
  ["what is a baby kangaroo called | names of baby animals | what are baby animals called", "Baby animals have special names: a baby kangaroo or koala is a **joey**, a baby goat is a **kid**, a baby swan is a **cygnet**, a baby owl is an **owlet**, a baby frog is a **tadpole**, and baby foxes, bears and lions are all **cubs**."],
  ["can penguins live in the arctic | are there penguins at the north pole", "No, wild penguins live almost entirely in the Southern Hemisphere, so you won't find them at the North Pole. Polar bears live in the Arctic, so polar bears and penguins never meet in the wild."],
  ["is a tomato a fruit or a vegetable | is tomato a fruit", "Botanically, a tomato is a **fruit** because it grows from a flower and contains seeds. In cooking it's treated like a vegetable because it's savory. Cucumbers, peppers and pumpkins are botanical fruits too!"],
  ["why do leaves change color | why do leaves turn orange in fall | why do leaves change color in autumn", "Leaves are green because of **chlorophyll**, which plants use to make food from sunlight. In autumn, days get shorter and cooler, so trees stop making chlorophyll. As the green fades, yellow and orange pigments that were there all along show through, and some trees also make red and purple pigments."],
  ["why do trees lose their leaves | why do leaves fall off trees", "Many trees drop their leaves in autumn to save water and energy during winter, when there's less sunlight and the ground may be frozen. These are called **deciduous** trees. Trees that keep their leaves all year, like pines, are called **evergreens**."],
  ["how do plants make food | what is photosynthesis", "Plants make their own food through **photosynthesis**. They take in sunlight with their leaves, water through their roots and carbon dioxide from the air, then turn them into sugar for energy. As a bonus, they release oxygen, which we breathe."],
  ["why are plants green | why are leaves green", "Plants look green because of **chlorophyll**, a pigment that absorbs red and blue light for photosynthesis and reflects green light back to our eyes."],
  ["what do plants need to grow | how do i keep a plant alive", "Most plants need **light, water, air, nutrients from soil and the right temperature**. To keep a houseplant happy, put it where it gets suitable light, water when the top of the soil feels dry (not on a strict schedule), and make sure the pot has drainage holes so roots don't sit in water."],
  ["what is the tallest tree | tallest tree in the world | tallest type of tree", "**Coast redwoods** in California are the tallest trees on Earth, with the tallest known ones over 110 m (more than 360 ft), taller than a 30-storey building."],
  ["what is the oldest tree | oldest tree in the world | how old can trees get", "Some trees live for thousands of years. Great Basin **bristlecone pines** in the western United States are among the oldest individual trees, at more than 4,000 years old. Some tree colonies that share one root system are even older."],
  ["what is the biggest tree | largest tree in the world | what is the biggest tree by volume", "By volume, the biggest single trees are **giant sequoias** in California. They have enormous, thick trunks and can weigh over a thousand tonnes."],
  ["how do you tell how old a tree is | how to count tree rings", "You can tell a tree's age by counting its **growth rings** on a cut stump. Most trees add one ring each year: a light band in spring and a darker band later in the season. Wide rings mean good growing years and narrow rings mean harder ones."],
  ["how do seeds grow | how does a seed turn into a plant", "A seed contains a tiny baby plant and a store of food. When it gets water, warmth and air, it **germinates**: a root grows down, a shoot grows up toward the light, and once leaves appear the plant can start making its own food through photosynthesis."],
  ["why do flowers smell nice | why do flowers have colors", "Flowers use bright colors and sweet scents to attract pollinators like bees, butterflies, birds and bats. In return for nectar, these visitors carry pollen from flower to flower, which helps plants make seeds."],
  ["what is pollination | how does pollination work", "Pollination is when pollen moves from the male part of a flower to the female part, so the plant can make seeds and fruit. Wind, water, insects, birds and bats can all carry pollen."],
  ["what is a cactus | how do cactus survive in the desert", "Cacti are plants built for dry places. They store water in thick, fleshy stems, and their spines are actually modified leaves that lose less water and protect them from thirsty animals. Their roots spread wide and shallow to soak up rain quickly."],
  ["what is a venus flytrap | do plants eat bugs | carnivorous plants", "A Venus flytrap is a **carnivorous plant** that catches insects. Its leaves snap shut when tiny trigger hairs are touched twice in a short time. It grows in poor soil, so the bugs give it extra nutrients. Pitcher plants and sundews are other bug-eating plants."],
  ["what is the biggest flower | largest flower in the world", "The **Rafflesia arnoldii** has the largest single flower, up to about 1 m (3 ft) across. It smells like rotting meat to attract flies! The titan arum has a giant flower structure too, and is also very stinky."],
  ["are mushrooms plants | what is a mushroom", "Mushrooms are not plants; they belong to a separate group called **fungi**. They can't make food from sunlight, so they feed on dead or living material. The mushroom we see is just the fruiting part; most of the fungus lives underground as a web of threads. Never eat wild mushrooms unless an expert says they're safe."],
  ["what is the fastest growing plant | how fast does bamboo grow", "Some kinds of **bamboo** are the fastest-growing plants, able to grow close to a metre in a single day in ideal conditions. Bamboo is actually a giant grass."],
  ["what is a rainforest | why are rainforests important", "Rainforests are warm, wet forests that get lots of rain all year. They cover a small part of Earth but are home to a huge share of its plant and animal species. They also store carbon, help make rain and give us foods and medicines, which is why protecting them matters."],
  ["how do trees help the environment | why are trees important", "Trees clean the air by taking in carbon dioxide and giving out oxygen. They provide homes and food for wildlife, cool cities with shade, hold soil in place with their roots and help prevent flooding."],
  ["why do sunflowers follow the sun | do sunflowers turn to face the sun", "Young sunflowers turn to follow the sun across the sky during the day, then swing back east at night. This is called **heliotropism**. Once they're fully grown and flowering, most end up facing east, where morning sun warms them and attracts more bees."],
  ["how do you play soccer | soccer rules | football rules | how to play football", "In soccer (called football in most of the world), two teams of **11 players** try to kick a ball into the other team's goal. Only the goalkeeper can use their hands, and only inside their own penalty area. A standard match is two halves of 45 minutes, and the team with more goals wins."],
  ["how many players are on a soccer team | how many players in a football team", "Each soccer team has **11 players** on the field at a time, including one goalkeeper. Teams also have substitutes on the bench who can come on during the game."],
  ["what is offside in soccer | offside rule explained", "A player is **offside** if, when a teammate passes the ball to them, they are in the opponent's half and closer to the goal line than both the ball and the second-to-last defender (usually the last outfield player). It stops players from just waiting next to the goal. You can't be offside from a throw-in, goal kick or corner."],
  ["how do you play basketball | basketball rules | how to play basketball", "In basketball, two teams of **5 players** try to throw a ball through the other team's hoop. Players move the ball by dribbling (bouncing it) or passing; you can't run while holding it. A basket is usually worth 2 points, a shot from beyond the three-point line is worth 3, and a free throw is worth 1."],
  ["how many players are on a basketball team | how many players on a basketball court", "Each basketball team has **5 players** on the court at a time: usually guards, forwards and a center. Teams can make substitutions during stoppages."],
  ["how do you play baseball | baseball rules | how to play baseball", "In baseball, two teams of **9 players** take turns batting and fielding. The pitcher throws the ball and the batter tries to hit it, then run around four bases. Reaching home plate scores a run. After three outs, the teams swap. A standard game has 9 innings, and the team with more runs wins."],
  ["what is a home run | what is a strike in baseball", "A **home run** is when a batter hits the ball and goes around all the bases to score in one go, often by hitting it over the outfield fence. A **strike** is a good pitch the batter misses or doesn't swing at; three strikes and the batter is out."],
  ["how do you play american football | american football rules | nfl rules explained", "In American football, two teams of **11 players** try to carry or throw an oval ball into the other team's end zone. The attacking team gets four tries (called downs) to move the ball 10 yards; if they make it they get four more. A touchdown is worth 6 points, followed by a kick for 1 or a play for 2, and a field goal is worth 3."],
  ["how do you play tennis | tennis rules | how does tennis scoring work", "In tennis, players hit a ball over a net with rackets, trying to make it land in the court so the opponent can't return it. Points go **15, 30, 40, game**; at 40-40 it's called deuce and you need two points in a row to win. Six games (winning by two) win a set, and matches are usually best of three or five sets. A score of zero is called love."],
  ["how do you play golf | golf rules | how to play golf", "In golf, players hit a small ball with clubs into a series of holes, usually 18, using as few strokes as possible. Each hole has a **par**, the number of strokes a good player is expected to take. One under par is a birdie, two under is an eagle, and one over is a bogey. The lowest total score wins."],
  ["how do you play hockey | ice hockey rules | how to play ice hockey", "In ice hockey, two teams of **6 players** (usually 5 skaters and a goalie) skate on ice and use sticks to shoot a rubber puck into the other team's net. Games are usually three periods of 20 minutes. Players can be sent to the penalty box for breaking rules."],
  ["how do you play field hockey | field hockey rules", "Field hockey is played on grass or turf by two teams of **11 players**, including a goalkeeper. Players use hooked sticks to pass and dribble a small hard ball, and they can only hit it with the flat side of the stick. Goals must be scored from inside the shooting circle."],
  ["how do you play volleyball | volleyball rules | how to play volleyball", "In volleyball, two teams of **6 players** hit a ball over a high net with their hands and arms. Each team can touch the ball up to three times before sending it over, usually a bump, set and spike. A point is scored when the ball hits the floor on the other side, and sets are usually played to 25."],
  ["how does swimming work as a sport | swimming strokes | what are the four swimming strokes", "Competitive swimming has four main strokes: **freestyle** (front crawl), **backstroke**, **breaststroke** and **butterfly**. Races are held over set distances in a pool, and the swimmer who touches the wall first wins. The individual medley uses all four strokes in one race."],
  ["how do you play chess | chess rules | how to play chess", "Chess is a game for two players on an 8x8 board, each starting with 16 pieces: a king, a queen, two rooks, two bishops, two knights and eight pawns. White moves first, and players take turns. The goal is **checkmate**: attacking the opponent's king so it has no safe move."],
  ["how do chess pieces move | how does the knight move in chess", "In chess: the **king** moves one square any way; the **queen** moves any distance in any direction; **rooks** move straight along rows and columns; **bishops** move diagonally; **knights** jump in an L shape (two squares one way, one square sideways); **pawns** move forward one square (two on their first move) and capture diagonally."],
  ["how do i get better at chess | chess tips for beginners", "Some good chess habits: control the center early, develop your knights and bishops before moving your queen out, castle to keep your king safe, and before every move ask \"what is my opponent threatening?\" Solving chess puzzles and reviewing your games to learn from mistakes are great ways to improve."],
  ["what is castling in chess | what is checkmate", "**Castling** is a special move where the king moves two squares toward a rook and the rook hops to the other side of the king, if neither has moved and there's nothing in between. **Checkmate** means the king is under attack and can't escape, which ends the game."],
  ["how do you play cricket | cricket rules | how to play cricket", "In cricket, two teams of **11 players** take turns batting and bowling. The bowler throws the ball at the wicket and the batter tries to hit it and score runs by running between the wickets, or by hitting it to the boundary for 4 or 6. A batter is out if, for example, the wicket is hit or the ball is caught. The team with the most runs wins."],
  ["how do you play rugby | rugby rules", "In rugby, two teams carry, pass and kick an oval ball, trying to touch it down over the opponent's goal line for a **try**. Passes must go backwards or sideways, never forward. Rugby union has 15 players per side and rugby league has 13."],
  ["how do you play badminton | badminton rules", "Badminton is played with rackets and a feathered shuttlecock hit over a high net, in singles or doubles. You score a point whenever the shuttle lands in your opponent's court or they make a mistake. Games are usually played to 21 points."],
  ["how do you play table tennis | ping pong rules | how to play ping pong", "In table tennis (ping pong), players hit a light ball over a low net on a table using small paddles. The ball must bounce once on the opponent's side. Games are usually played to 11 points, and you must win by 2."],
  ["what is a marathon | how long is a marathon", "A marathon is a running race of **42.195 km (26.2 miles)**. It's named after a legend about a Greek messenger who ran from the town of Marathon to Athens."],
  ["what is a triathlon | what sports are in a triathlon", "A triathlon is a race made of three back-to-back sports: **swimming, cycling and running**. Distances vary, from short sprint triathlons to very long ones."],
  ["what are the olympics | what is the olympic games | tell me about the olympics", "The Olympic Games are the world's biggest international sports competition, where athletes from almost every country compete for gold, silver and bronze medals. They were inspired by ancient games held in Olympia, Greece, and the modern Olympics began in Athens in 1896."],
  ["how often are the olympics | how often do the olympics happen | when do the olympics happen", "The Summer and Winter Olympics each take place **every four years**, staggered so there's an Olympic Games every two years. The four-year gap is called an **Olympiad**."],
  ["what do the olympic rings mean | why are there five olympic rings", "The five interlocking Olympic rings stand for the union of the five inhabited continents taking part in the Games. Their colors (blue, yellow, black, green and red) on a white background were chosen because every nation's flag contained at least one of them."],
  ["what is the difference between summer and winter olympics | winter olympics sports", "The Summer Olympics include sports like athletics, swimming, gymnastics and cycling. The Winter Olympics feature snow and ice sports, such as skiing, snowboarding, figure skating, ice hockey, curling and bobsleigh."],
  ["what are the paralympics | tell me about the paralympics", "The Paralympic Games are a major international competition for athletes with disabilities, held shortly after the Olympics in the same host city. They include sports like wheelchair basketball, para swimming, goalball and para athletics."],
  ["what is the olympic torch | why is there an olympic flame", "The Olympic flame is lit in Olympia, Greece, using the sun's rays and a mirror, then carried in a torch relay to the host city. It burns throughout the Games as a symbol of peace and the link to the ancient Olympics."],
  ["what is the most popular sport in the world | most played sport", "**Soccer** (association football) is widely considered the most popular sport in the world, played and watched in nearly every country. Cricket, basketball and tennis also have huge numbers of fans."],
  ["how can i get better at sports | how to improve at sports", "Practise the basic skills regularly, even just 15 minutes a day. Work on fitness with running, stretching and strength exercises, watch skilled players to learn technique, and ask a coach or teammate for feedback. Rest, sleep, water and good food matter too, and most of all, have fun!"],
  ["why should i warm up before exercise | why is stretching important", "Warming up gets your heart pumping and your muscles warm and flexible, which helps you perform better and lowers the chance of injury. A good warm-up is 5 to 10 minutes of light movement like jogging or jumping jacks, followed by moving stretches."],
  ["what is a hat trick | what does hat trick mean", "A **hat trick** is when one player scores three goals in a single game, used in soccer, hockey and other sports. The term started in cricket, for a bowler taking three wickets with three balls in a row."],
  ["how do you play dodgeball | dodgeball rules", "In dodgeball, two teams throw soft balls at each other, trying to hit opponents. If you're hit you're out, but if you catch a thrown ball the thrower is out and often a teammate gets back in. The team that gets everyone on the other side out wins."],
  ["how do you play tag | rules of tag", "In tag, one player is \"it\" and chases the others. When they touch someone, that person becomes \"it.\" Many versions add a \"safe\" base where you can't be tagged, or freeze tag, where tagged players freeze until a teammate frees them."],
  ["how do you play hide and seek | hide and seek rules", "One player is the seeker and covers their eyes while counting (say, to 20), while everyone else hides. Then the seeker searches. The first person found is often the next seeker. Remember to pick safe hiding spots and agree on the play area!"],
  ["what are video game genres | types of video games | kinds of video games", "Some popular video game genres:\n- **Action / adventure**: exploring, fighting and solving puzzles\n- **Platformer**: running and jumping across levels\n- **RPG** (role-playing game): building a character and following a story\n- **Strategy**: planning and managing armies or cities\n- **Puzzle**: brain-teasers and logic\n- **Shooter**, **racing**, **sports**, **simulation** and **sandbox** games too!"],
  ["what is an rpg | what does rpg mean", "**RPG** stands for role-playing game. You control a character (or a party), level up by gaining experience, collect gear, make choices and follow a story. Some are turn-based, others are action-packed."],
  ["what is a platformer game | what is a platform game", "A platformer is a game where you guide a character through levels by running, jumping and climbing between platforms while avoiding hazards and enemies. Timing and precise jumps are the key skills."],
  ["what is a sandbox game | what is an open world game", "A **sandbox** game gives you tools and freedom to build, explore and create your own goals instead of following one fixed path. An **open world** game lets you roam a big map freely and choose where to go next."],
  ["what is a strategy game | what is rts", "Strategy games are about planning and smart decisions: managing resources, building bases and directing units. **RTS** means real-time strategy, where everything happens at once; turn-based strategy lets players take turns like in chess."],
  ["what is a roguelike | what does roguelike mean", "A **roguelike** is a game where each run is different (levels are randomly generated) and when you lose, you usually start over from the beginning. Many modern ones, sometimes called roguelites, let you unlock permanent upgrades between runs."],
  ["what does npc mean | what is an npc in games", "**NPC** stands for non-player character: any character in a game controlled by the computer instead of a player, like shopkeepers, villagers and quest-givers."],
  ["what does gg mean | what does gg mean in games", "**GG** means \"good game.\" Players say it at the end of a match to show good sportsmanship, whether they won or lost."],
  ["what does afk mean | what does noob mean", "**AFK** means \"away from keyboard,\" when a player has stepped away. **Noob** (or newbie) means someone who is new to a game. Everyone starts as a newbie, so be kind to them!"],
  ["what does hp mean in games | what is xp in games", "**HP** means hit points or health points: how much damage your character can take. **XP** means experience points, which you earn by playing and use to level up and get stronger."],
  ["how do i get better at video games | gaming tips | how to improve at games", "Some tips to level up your gaming:\n- Learn the controls and practise basic moves until they feel natural\n- Watch how skilled players handle tricky parts\n- Focus on one skill at a time, like aiming or timing\n- Review what went wrong after you lose\n- Adjust settings like sensitivity to suit you\n- Take breaks so you stay sharp and don't get frustrated"],
  ["how do i beat a hard boss | i can't beat this level | tips for hard levels", "Stuck on a tough part? Watch the boss's attack patterns and look for openings after big attacks. Upgrade your gear or level up a bit, try a different strategy or weapon, and take a short break if you're frustrated; a fresh mind often wins. Looking up a guide is fine too!"],
  ["how long should i play video games | how much gaming is too much", "There's no single rule, but it's good to balance gaming with sleep, homework, being active and time with family and friends. Taking a short break every hour to stretch and rest your eyes helps. If you're a kid, follow the screen-time rules your grown-ups set."],
  ["how do i stay safe playing online games | online gaming safety", "Stay safe online by never sharing personal info like your real name, address, school or passwords. Be kind, use block and report tools if someone is mean, and tell a trusted adult if anything makes you uncomfortable. Be careful about in-game purchases and ask permission first."],
  ["what is a speedrun | what is speedrunning", "Speedrunning is trying to finish a game as fast as possible. Speedrunners learn every shortcut, practise perfect moves and sometimes use clever glitches. Many share their runs online for others to watch."],
  ["what is the first video game | what was the first video game ever", "Early computer games were made in the 1950s and 1960s, like **Tennis for Two** (1958) and **Spacewar!** (1962). **Pong** (1972), a simple table tennis game, was one of the first arcade hits and helped start the video game industry."],
  ["what is esports | what are esports", "Esports are organised video game competitions where players or teams compete, often in front of big audiences online or in arenas. Popular esports genres include fighting games, strategy games, sports games and team shooters."],
  ["what is a good board game | recommend a board game | fun board games to play", "Some classic board games: **chess** and **checkers** for strategy, **Scrabble** for word lovers, **Monopoly** for trading, **Clue** for mystery solving and **Snakes and Ladders** for younger players. Card games like Uno are great fun too!"],
  ["how do you play checkers | checkers rules | draughts rules", "In checkers (draughts), each player starts with 12 pieces on the dark squares. Pieces move diagonally forward one square and capture by jumping over an opponent's piece. If a piece reaches the far side it becomes a **king** and can move backwards too. You win by capturing or blocking all the opponent's pieces."],
  ["how do you play tic tac toe | tic tac toe tips", "Tic-tac-toe is played on a 3x3 grid. Players take turns placing X or O, and the first to get three in a row (across, down or diagonal) wins. Tip: take the center or a corner first. If both players play perfectly, the game always ends in a draw!"],
  ["how do you play rock paper scissors | rock paper scissors rules", "On the count of three, both players show rock (fist), paper (flat hand) or scissors (two fingers). **Rock** crushes scissors, **scissors** cut paper, and **paper** covers rock. Same choice? Go again!"],
  ["how do i get better at drawing | how to draw better | drawing tips", "Drawing gets better with practice! Try these:\n- Sketch something every day, even for 10 minutes\n- Draw from real life: objects, pets, people\n- Break things down into simple shapes like circles, boxes and cylinders\n- Study light and shadow\n- Use light lines first, then darken the ones you like\n- Keep old drawings so you can see how much you've improved"],
  ["how do i draw a person | how to draw people | how to draw a face", "Start simple: draw an oval for the head and a stick figure to set the pose, then add shapes for the body and limbs. For faces, the eyes sit about halfway down the head, the nose about halfway between the eyes and chin, and the mouth a little below the nose. Light construction lines help a lot."],
  ["what should i draw | give me something to draw | drawing ideas", "Here are some drawing ideas: a cat wearing a tiny hat, a treehouse city, your dream bedroom, a dragon made of clouds, a robot chef, an underwater castle, your favourite food with a face, or a map of an imaginary island. Pick one and go!"],
  ["what are the primary colors | what are the 3 primary colors", "In traditional painting, the primary colors are **red, yellow and blue**. You can't mix them from other colors, but you can mix them to make many others. For light on screens, the primary colors are **red, green and blue** (RGB)."],
  ["what are secondary colors | what colors make secondary colors", "Secondary colors are made by mixing two primary paint colors:\n- Red + yellow = **orange**\n- Yellow + blue = **green**\n- Blue + red = **purple**"],
  ["what colors make green | how do you make green", "Mix **blue and yellow** paint to make green. More yellow gives a bright lime green, and more blue gives a deeper green."],
  ["what colors make purple | how do you make purple", "Mix **red and blue** paint to make purple. Add a little white for a lighter lavender, or more blue for a cooler violet."],
  ["what colors make orange | how do you make orange", "Mix **red and yellow** paint to make orange. More yellow makes a lighter, sunny orange; more red makes a deep reddish orange."],
  ["what colors make brown | how do you make brown", "Brown can be made by mixing all three primary colors (red, yellow and blue) together, or by mixing a color with its complementary color, like orange and blue. Adjust with a bit more red or yellow to warm it up."],
  ["what colors make pink | how do you make pink", "Mix **red and white** paint to make pink. The more white you add, the lighter and softer the pink becomes."],
  ["how do you make black paint | what colors make black", "You can mix a very dark, near-black by combining deep versions of the primaries or complementary colors, like dark blue with brown or red with green. Many artists like these mixed darks because they look richer than plain black."],
  ["what are complementary colors | what is the color wheel", "The **color wheel** arranges colors in a circle to show how they relate. **Complementary colors** sit opposite each other, like red and green, blue and orange, or yellow and purple. Placed side by side they make each other pop; mixed together they make a dull brown or grey."],
  ["what are warm and cool colors | warm colors vs cool colors", "**Warm colors** are reds, oranges and yellows; they feel energetic and seem to come forward. **Cool colors** are blues, greens and purples; they feel calm and seem to sink back. Artists use them to set the mood of a picture."],
  ["what colors make up white light | what are the colors of the rainbow", "White light is made of all the colors of the rainbow mixed together. The rainbow's colors in order are **red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet**, which you can remember with the name **ROY G BIV**."],
  ["tell me about the mona lisa | who painted the mona lisa | mona lisa facts", "The **Mona Lisa** was painted by **Leonardo da Vinci** in the early 1500s and hangs in the Louvre museum in Paris. It's famous for the woman's mysterious smile and for Leonardo's soft shading technique called **sfumato**. It's actually fairly small, only about 77 x 53 cm."],
  ["tell me about the starry night | who painted the starry night | starry night facts", "**The Starry Night** was painted by **Vincent van Gogh** in 1889. It shows a swirling night sky over a quiet village, with a tall cypress tree in front. Van Gogh painted it from memory and imagination based on the view from his window. It's kept at the Museum of Modern Art in New York."],
  ["who was vincent van gogh | tell me about van gogh", "Vincent van Gogh (1853-1890) was a Dutch painter famous for bright colors and thick, swirling brushstrokes. His well-known works include **The Starry Night**, **Sunflowers** and his many self-portraits. He sold very few paintings in his lifetime but is now one of the most famous artists ever."],
  ["who was leonardo da vinci | tell me about leonardo da vinci", "Leonardo da Vinci (1452-1519) was an Italian Renaissance artist and inventor. He painted the **Mona Lisa** and **The Last Supper**, and filled notebooks with drawings of anatomy, machines and flying devices centuries ahead of their time."],
  ["who was michelangelo | tell me about michelangelo", "Michelangelo (1475-1564) was an Italian Renaissance sculptor, painter and architect. He carved the famous statue of **David** and painted the ceiling of the **Sistine Chapel** in Vatican City, lying on scaffolding for years to finish it."],
  ["who was pablo picasso | tell me about picasso", "Pablo Picasso (1881-1973) was a Spanish artist and one of the most influential of the 20th century. He helped invent **Cubism**, a style that shows objects from several angles at once using geometric shapes. His famous works include **Guernica**."],
  ["who was claude monet | tell me about monet | what is impressionism", "Claude Monet (1840-1926) was a French painter and a founder of **Impressionism**, a style that captures light and color with quick, visible brushstrokes. He's famous for his **Water Lilies** paintings of his garden pond in Giverny."],
  ["who was frida kahlo | tell me about frida kahlo", "Frida Kahlo (1907-1954) was a Mexican painter famous for her bold, colorful self-portraits filled with symbols from Mexican culture and her own life. She often painted herself with flowers in her hair and animals like monkeys and parrots."],
  ["what is the scream painting | who painted the scream", "**The Scream** was created by Norwegian artist **Edvard Munch** in 1893. It shows a figure clutching its face under a swirling orange sky and has become a famous symbol of anxiety. Munch made several versions of it."],
  ["who painted the girl with a pearl earring | tell me about vermeer", "**Girl with a Pearl Earring** was painted by the Dutch artist **Johannes Vermeer** around 1665. Vermeer is known for his calm scenes of everyday life and beautiful use of soft light."],
  ["what are some famous paintings | most famous paintings in the world", "Some of the most famous paintings: **Mona Lisa** (Leonardo da Vinci), **The Starry Night** (Van Gogh), **The Scream** (Munch), **Girl with a Pearl Earring** (Vermeer), **The Last Supper** (Leonardo), **The Birth of Venus** (Botticelli), **The Persistence of Memory** with its melting clocks (Dalí) and **Water Lilies** (Monet)."],
  ["what is abstract art | what is modern art", "**Abstract art** doesn't try to show things as they look in real life. Instead it uses shapes, colors, lines and textures to express feelings or ideas. Artists like Wassily Kandinsky and Piet Mondrian were abstract pioneers."],
  ["what is the difference between watercolor and acrylic paint | types of paint", "**Watercolor** is thin and see-through, mixed with water, and great for soft washes. **Acrylic** dries fast and can be used thick or thin, and it's water-resistant once dry. **Oil paint** dries slowly, which lets artists blend colors smoothly over days. **Gouache** is like an opaque watercolor."],
  ["what is a sculpture | what is sculpting", "A sculpture is a three-dimensional artwork made by carving, modelling or building materials like stone, wood, clay, metal or even recycled objects. Famous examples include Michelangelo's **David** and Rodin's **The Thinker**."],
  ["what is the louvre | what is the biggest art museum", "The **Louvre** in Paris, France, is one of the largest and most visited art museums in the world. It was once a royal palace and is home to the **Mona Lisa** and the ancient statue **Venus de Milo**. Its glass pyramid entrance is famous too."],
  ["what are music notes | what are the musical notes | what are the 7 notes", "Western music uses seven note names: **A, B, C, D, E, F and G**, which repeat in higher and lower octaves. Some people sing them as **do, re, mi, fa, sol, la, ti**. Adding sharps (#) and flats (b) gives 12 different notes in total."],
  ["what is rhythm | what is rhythm in music", "**Rhythm** is the pattern of long and short sounds and silences in music, the part that makes you want to tap your foot or clap. It sits on top of the **beat**, the steady pulse that keeps time."],
  ["what is melody | what is a melody in music", "A **melody** is a series of notes played one after another that makes a tune, the part you hum or sing along to. It's like the main storyline of a song."],
  ["what is harmony | what is harmony in music", "**Harmony** is when different notes are played or sung at the same time to support the melody. Chords are a big part of harmony, and they give music its mood, from happy to sad or tense."],
  ["what is tempo | what is a beat in music", "The **beat** is the steady pulse of music, like a heartbeat. **Tempo** is how fast that beat goes, often measured in beats per minute (BPM). A slow ballad might be 60 to 80 BPM, while a fast dance song might be 120 or more."],
  ["what is a chord | what is a music chord", "A **chord** is three or more notes played together. A simple chord, called a triad, uses a root note plus the third and fifth notes above it. Major chords tend to sound bright and happy, and minor chords sound more sad or serious."],
  ["what is the difference between major and minor | major vs minor key", "Major and minor are two kinds of scales and chords. Music in a **major** key often sounds bright, cheerful and confident, while music in a **minor** key often sounds darker, sadder or more mysterious. The difference comes from a few notes being one half-step lower in minor."],
  ["what is an octave | what does octave mean", "An **octave** is the distance between one note and the next note with the same name, higher or lower, like from one C to the next C. The higher note vibrates exactly twice as fast as the lower one, which is why they sound so similar."],
  ["what are the families of musical instruments | types of musical instruments | instrument families", "Instruments are often grouped into families:\n- **Strings**: violin, cello, guitar, harp\n- **Woodwinds**: flute, clarinet, oboe, saxophone\n- **Brass**: trumpet, trombone, French horn, tuba\n- **Percussion**: drums, xylophone, cymbals\n- **Keyboards**: piano, organ"],
  ["what instrument should i learn | what is the easiest instrument to learn | best instrument for beginners", "Great starter instruments include the **piano or keyboard** (you can see all the notes laid out), the **ukulele** (only four strings and easy chords), and the **recorder**. Pick the one you're most excited to play, because enjoying it keeps you practising."],
  ["how do i start learning piano | how to learn piano | piano tips for beginners", "To start piano:\n- Learn where the notes are; C is the white key just left of the group of two black keys\n- Sit up straight with curved fingers\n- Practise simple songs with your right hand, then your left, then both together\n- Learn to read basic notes and rhythm\n- Practise a little every day, even 15 minutes beats one long session a week"],
  ["how do i start learning guitar | how to learn guitar | guitar tips for beginners", "To start guitar:\n- Learn to tune it (a tuner app or clip-on tuner helps)\n- Start with easy chords like **G, C, D, E minor and A minor**\n- Practise switching between two chords slowly, then faster\n- Learn a simple strumming pattern\n- Your fingertips will feel sore at first, but they toughen up in a couple of weeks\n- Play songs you love to stay motivated"],
  ["how many keys are on a piano | how many keys does a piano have", "A standard full-size piano has **88 keys**: 52 white keys and 36 black keys."],
  ["how many strings does a guitar have | how many strings on a violin", "A standard guitar has **6 strings**, usually tuned E, A, D, G, B, E from lowest to highest. A violin has **4 strings** (G, D, A, E), and a ukulele also has 4."],
  ["how do i read sheet music | how to read music notes", "Sheet music uses five lines called a **staff**. Notes placed higher on the staff sound higher. In the treble clef, the lines from bottom to top are **E, G, B, D, F** (\"Every Good Boy Does Fine\") and the spaces spell **F, A, C, E**. The shape of a note shows how long to hold it: whole, half, quarter and eighth notes."],
  ["how do i learn to sing | singing tips | how to sing better", "To sing better, stand tall and breathe from your belly, not your shoulders. Warm up with humming and gentle scales, sing along with songs you love, and record yourself to hear what to improve. Drink water and never strain or shout; singing should feel comfortable."],
  ["how do i practice an instrument | how to practice music", "Short, focused practice every day works best. Start with a warm-up, then work on the hardest bit slowly before speeding up. Use a metronome to keep steady time, and finish by playing something fun you already know."],
  ["who was mozart | tell me about mozart", "Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart (1756-1791) was an Austrian composer and a child prodigy who wrote music from age five. In his short life he composed over 600 works, including symphonies, operas like **The Magic Flute** and the famous **Eine kleine Nachtmusik**."],
  ["who was beethoven | tell me about beethoven", "Ludwig van Beethoven (1770-1827) was a German composer who bridged the Classical and Romantic eras. Amazingly, he kept composing even after he lost his hearing. His famous works include the **Fifth Symphony**, the **Ninth Symphony** with the \"Ode to Joy,\" and **Für Elise**."],
  ["who was bach | tell me about bach", "Johann Sebastian Bach (1685-1750) was a German composer of the Baroque era, known for complex, beautiful music where several melodies weave together. Famous works include the **Brandenburg Concertos** and the **Toccata and Fugue in D minor**."],
  ["who was vivaldi | what is the four seasons", "Antonio Vivaldi (1678-1741) was an Italian Baroque composer and violinist. His best-known work, **The Four Seasons**, is a set of four violin concertos that paint musical pictures of spring, summer, autumn and winter."],
  ["who was tchaikovsky | who wrote the nutcracker", "Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky (1840-1893) was a Russian composer famous for his emotional melodies. He wrote the music for the ballets **The Nutcracker**, **Swan Lake** and **Sleeping Beauty**, as well as the **1812 Overture** with its booming cannons."],
  ["who was chopin | tell me about chopin", "Frédéric Chopin (1810-1849) was a Polish composer and pianist who wrote almost all his music for piano. His nocturnes, waltzes and études are loved for their poetic, expressive style."],
  ["who are famous composers | best classical composers | famous classical musicians", "Some of the most famous classical composers: **Bach**, **Vivaldi**, **Mozart**, **Beethoven**, **Chopin**, **Tchaikovsky**, **Brahms**, **Debussy** and **Haydn**. Listening to a few of their best-known pieces is a great way to start exploring classical music."],
  ["what is an orchestra | how many people are in an orchestra", "An orchestra is a large group of musicians playing strings, woodwinds, brass and percussion together, led by a **conductor**. A full symphony orchestra can have around 70 to 100 players."],
  ["what does a conductor do | why do orchestras have conductors", "A conductor leads an orchestra by setting the tempo with a baton or hands, cueing when sections come in and shaping how loud or soft and how expressive the music is. They also lead rehearsals so everyone plays as one."],
  ["what is a symphony | what is an opera", "A **symphony** is a large piece for orchestra, usually in several sections called movements. An **opera** is a story told through music, where performers sing their parts with costumes, sets and an orchestra."],
  ["what are the genres of music | types of music | music genres", "Popular music genres include **pop, rock, hip-hop, jazz, classical, country, electronic, R&B, reggae, blues, folk and metal**. Many songs blend genres, so there's always something new to discover."],
  ["what is jazz | where did jazz come from", "Jazz is a style of music that began in African American communities in New Orleans in the late 1800s and early 1900s. It's known for swing rhythms, blues notes and **improvisation**, where musicians make up solos on the spot."],
  ["why do we get songs stuck in our heads | what is an earworm", "A song stuck in your head is called an **earworm**. Catchy, simple, repetitive tunes are the most likely culprits, and your brain replays them to \"finish\" them. Listening to the whole song or doing a puzzle can help shake it loose."],
  ["why is the sky blue | why does the sky look blue", "Sunlight contains all colors. When it hits the air, the tiny gas molecules scatter blue light much more than red light, so blue light bounces all over the sky and reaches our eyes from every direction. At sunset, light travels through more air, so we see more reds and oranges."],
  ["how do rainbows form | why do rainbows happen", "Rainbows appear when sunlight shines through raindrops. Each drop bends and reflects the light, splitting it into its colors. To see one, stand with the sun behind you and rain in front of you."],
  ["why do we yawn | why are yawns contagious", "Scientists aren't completely sure why we yawn, but one idea is that it helps cool the brain and keeps us alert. Yawns are contagious, possibly because of how social we are; even reading about yawning can make you yawn!"],
  ["why do we dream | what are dreams", "Dreams happen mostly during **REM sleep**, a stage when your brain is very active. Scientists think dreaming may help us process memories and emotions, though there's still a lot of mystery about it."],
  ["how many bones are in the human body | how many bones do adults have", "An adult human has **206 bones**. Babies are born with around 270, but some fuse together as they grow. The smallest bone is in your ear, and the longest is your thigh bone, the femur."],
  ["why do onions make you cry | why do i cry when cutting onions", "Cutting an onion breaks its cells and releases a gas that stings your eyes, so your eyes make tears to wash it away. Chilling the onion first or using a sharp knife can help."],
  ["what is the hottest planet | which planet is the hottest", "**Venus** is the hottest planet, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun. Venus has a thick atmosphere of carbon dioxide that traps heat, making its surface hot enough to melt lead."],
  ["how many planets are in the solar system | name the planets", "There are **8 planets**: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune. A fun memory trick: \"My Very Easy Method Just Speeds Up Naming.\""],
  ["tell me a fun fact | give me a random fact | tell me something interesting", "Here's one: **honey never spoils**. Archaeologists have found pots of honey in ancient Egyptian tombs that were thousands of years old and still edible. Want another? Ask me about animals, art or music!"],
  ["tell me a fun animal fact | give me a cool animal fact | random animal fact", "Did you know a group of flamingos is called a **flamboyance**? Also, sea otters hold hands while they sleep, and cows have best friends. Animals are amazing!"],
  ["tell me a weird fact | tell me something surprising", "Bananas are botanically berries, but strawberries aren't! Strawberries have their seeds on the outside, which means botanists don't count them as true berries."],
  ["would you rather fly or be invisible | would you rather be able to fly or turn invisible", "Ooh, tough one! I'd pick **flying**: no traffic, amazing views and I could visit the clouds whenever I like. Invisibility sounds fun, but I'd probably just bump into things. What would you choose?"],
  ["would you rather be a cat or a dog | cats or dogs | are you a cat person or a dog person", "I'm a bit of both! Cats win at napping and looking elegant, but dogs win at enthusiasm and fetch. If I had to choose, maybe a cat; 16 hours of sleep a day sounds very relaxing for a busy computer. Which team are you on?"],
  ["would you rather live in the ocean or in space | would you rather explore the ocean or space", "I'd pick the **ocean**: there are glowing jellyfish, giant squid and parts of the deep sea nobody has ever seen. Space has amazing views, but the ocean has better snacks. What about you?"],
  ["would you rather talk to animals or speak every language | would you rather speak to animals", "Talking to animals, definitely! I'd love to know what cats are really thinking and what whales are singing about. Though every language would be pretty handy for travelling. Which would you pick?"],
  ["would you rather have a pet dragon or a pet unicorn | dragon or unicorn", "A **pet dragon**, no question: built-in heating in winter and the fastest ride to school ever. The vet bills might be a bit scary though. Unicorn fans, I respect you!"],
  ["would you rather be super strong or super fast | strength or speed", "I'd go with **super speed**: I could finish chores in a blink and never be late again. Super strength is cool too, but I'd worry about accidentally crushing my keyboard. What's your pick?"],
  ["would you rather have no homework or no chores | homework or chores", "That's a sneaky question! Skipping chores sounds great until there are no clean socks. I'd pick no homework, then use the free time to learn something fun. What about you?"],
  ["would you rather live without music or without games | music or video games", "Oof, that's hard! I think I'd keep **music**, because you can enjoy it while doing almost anything, even while playing games in your head. Which would you keep?"],
  ["would you rather be a giant or tiny | would you rather be huge or tiny", "I'd try **tiny** for a day: a leaf could be a boat, and a cat would look like a friendly dragon. Being a giant would make it hard to find shoes that fit! How about you?"],
  ["would you rather explore the jungle or the desert | jungle or desert", "The **jungle**! Toucans, sloths, giant leaves and waterfalls everywhere. The desert has beautiful stars at night though, so maybe I'd camp there afterwards."],
  ["ask me a would you rather question | give me a would you rather | would you rather game", "Okay, here's one: **Would you rather have a talking pet goldfish that only tells jokes, or a cat that sings opera every morning?** Tell me your answer and why!"],
  ["what is your favorite animal | do you have a favorite animal", "I love the **octopus**: three hearts, blue blood, color-changing skin and clever enough to open jars. But honestly, every animal is pretty amazing. What's your favorite?"],
  ["what is your favorite color | do you have a favorite color", "I'm a fan of **blue**, like a clear sky and a calm sea (and maybe a certain desktop wallpaper). What's your favorite color?"],
  ["what is your favorite song | do you like music", "I don't hear music the way you do, but I love talking about it! I find Beethoven's \"Ode to Joy\" pretty uplifting to read about. What do you like to listen to?"],
  ["what is your favorite game | do you play video games", "I can't hold a controller, but if I could, I'd probably play puzzle games and chess. I'd definitely lose at anything that needs fast reflexes! What do you like to play?"],
  ["what is your favorite sport | do you like sports", "I can't run or kick, but chess counts as a sport for my circuits! I also think the teamwork in soccer and basketball is really cool. Do you play any sports?"],
);
